core_ideas.explanation:
Economic statecraft means using state power on the economy to coerce other countries. Examples Ajay gives:
- the US blocking a Chinese purchase of American businesses and Dubai Ports World's purchase of a US port;
- export controls on top-end GPUs for China, and a ban on the Dutch firm ASML selling its machines to Chinese buyers (each costs around $150–200 million and prints chip tracks about ten silicon atoms wide);
- US research subsidies that bar recipients from building new facilities in China;
- the seizure of about $600 billion of the Russian central bank's reserves held abroad, and cutting Russian entities off from SWIFT.
The moral case is that choking an aggressor economically costs fewer lives than fighting with guns. The response to Russia is also meant for an audience of one, Xi Jinping, as a warning against invading Taiwan.
